Remember the relationship:

Site → Page → Web Parts

What is a Web Part?

A Web Part is a reusable UI component that you place inside a SharePoint Page.

For example, your Budget Approval Home Page could contain:

Text Web Part → instructions
List Web Part → displays Budget Requests
Document Library Web Part → displays Budget Documents
Quick Links Web Part → links/buttons
People Web Part → project members
News Web Part → announcements

So:
Page = screen/container
Web Part = component placed on that screen
Built-in vs Custom
There are two important categories:
    1. Built-in Web Parts
     Microsoft already provides them. You configure them through the UI. No coding required.
    
    2. Custom Web Parts
     If built-in components don't meet your requirement, a developer can create a custom one using SPFx, commonly with TypeScript/React.
    
  Example:

      Custom Budget Dashboard Web Part
       → calls SharePoint REST API
       → reads Budget Requests List
       → displays Pending / Approved / Rejected requests in your custom UI.
